[Music]  why does this fight scene work so well  is it because the underdogs come out on  top or because before the fight they  used to be friends well not really there  are three things that make up a good  superhero fight effective cinematography  audience investment and the right level  of realism and in this video we're gonna  talk about these three aspects of  superhero fights as well as how this  fight scene from captain america civil  war checks off all the boxes first  things first though we have to talk  about cinematography  [Music]  one thing a lot of superhero movies get  wrong during fight scenes is the  cinematography whether it be because of  an unnecessary shaky cam  incomprehensible flying fights or just  bad choreography a lot of big budget  superhero films fail to meet the mark  take the shaky cam technique for example  in some cases it can be used to  perfection such as in the church fight  scene and kingsman the secret service it  can help lend a sense that everything is  out of control in a frenzied craze or  like many scenes in the jason bourne  franchise it can make scenes almost  impossible to watch a lot of times it  seems shaky cam is simply used to cover  up poorly choreographed fight scenes  compare this scene from 2014's john wick  to this one from 2007's born ultimatum  in one we have a highly stylized brutal  fight sequence where it's hard to miss a  beat and the other we see rapid cuts and  a screen shaking so badly it's difficult  to see what's even happening  it's not too hard to tell that john wick  displays the epitome of what fight  scenes should look like and you can find  echoes of the techniques used in john  wick in several superhero fight scenes  tools such as shaky cam are just that  tools they can be used correctly or  incorrectly depending on the scene in  the film in which they're used another  issue that comes up a lot in regard to  superhero fight cinematography is the  infamous flying fight scene you know the  scene where two seemingly invincible  characters fly around bursting through  skyscrapers shooting lasers out of their  eyes at one another where it seems as if  nothing is ever really accomplished  these scenes can be painful to watch and  a lot of this comes from the fact that  we can't tell if any of what they're  going through is painful for them we can  only feel their pain when they're more  grounded and i mean that literally for  instance we can't tell if it hurts when  superman is flying through a building  with concrete falling down on his head  but we can certainly tell when iron man  sucker punches captain america that that  hurt when the heroes finally find  themselves on the ground that's when we  start to actually see what's happening  and can feel the weight behind some of  the hits that land in most flying fight  scenes it's hard to tell what's going on  and it starts to feel like unnecessary  fluff just there to make the run time a  bit longer when the fights come to the  ground though it's more likely that  we'll be able to feel what the  characters feel and it gets us more  invested in their struggles which brings  us to our next topic audience investment  you can typically tell when an audience  is invested in a fight scene if they  wince when a big blow is landed or when  a bone is broken on screen take this  scene from the dark knight rises when  bane slams bruce down on his knee the  audience cringes when they hear his  bones crack  now why is this why do we care when  christian bale's batman gets hurt but  less so when ben affleck's batman gets  hit it's because we're invested in  christian bale's version of batman it's  because we care about the outcome of his  fight and we want to know who wins but  how exactly does a film get an audience  invested in its characters well they  could establish a narrative across a  cinematic universe spanning over a  decade to get the audience invested like  we saw in avengers end game or they  could just give us sympathetic  characters with somewhat relatable  stories like we saw in captain america  civil war movies have to get their  audience invested in the characters to  make satisfying fight scenes an instance  where this certainly wasn't the case was  in the final fight scene in batman vs  superman i really doubt anyone walked  out of the movie saying they thought  that fight exceeded their expectations  the reason a lot of these scenes fail to  get us invested is because they lack  high stakes and by high stakes i don't  mean something like the end of the world  i simply mean that there has to be  something in jeopardy that's important  to the characters for us to care about  the outcome of the fight and again this  doesn't have to be something like a bomb  dropping on a major city or a giant  about to rip itself out of the earth's  core it can be something as simple as a  man trying to protect his best friend i  think this kind of grounded approach to  finding the right stakes for a given  situation is essential and it's rooted  in the film's level of realism over the  past decade we've seen superhero movies  try to create stories that feel more and  more realistic with varying levels of  success the thought is that older  superhero movies are too far-fetched for  people to believe in and care about the  heroes so creators have tried to make  their films as realistic as possible  while still trying to make us believe  that the characters in their films can  fly and lift cars above their heads  while this thought has some merit it  should be taken with a grain of salt one  of the reasons why so many people love  superhero movies is because they grant  us a sense of escapism and they allow us  to suspend our disbelief for a couple of  hours while we watch people and tights  beat each other up it's this suspension  of disbelief that makes superhero movies  superhero movies without it we'd be left  with no caped crusaders and no friendly  neighborhood bug heroes finding the  right level of realism for a superhero  movie can be hard though let's take the  character of batman for example in some  cases the realism in batman movies can  go too far like with 2016's batman vs  superman or not far enough like with  1997's batman and robin and others  though they can get it just right like  we saw in 2022's the batman this film  finds the perfect balance between  realism and escapism and a franchise  where it's rarely been found before the  batman gives us a realistic and  believable character while also allowing  us to suspend our disbelief and escape  our world into the dark gritty  underworld of gotham city very few  superhero stories have been able to find  this balance and it's difficult to make  a good superhero fight without it  however there are a few exceptions to  this rule for instance the tv show  invincible finds itself very low on the  realism spectrum but it's still able to  give us some amazing fight sequences  because we're invested in the characters  because we receive some stunning  animation and fight choreography and  simply because of the pure shock and awe  its scenes present on the opposite end  of the realism spectrum though we can  find the tv show the boys which also  gives us some incredible fight scenes  even in its hyper-realistic environment  again this is because of audience  investment great choreography and  cinematography and the show's fresh take  on the superhero genre i believe that  the best superhero fight scenes though  are the ones that do all of this they  use great cinematography they have a  high level of audience investment and  they find the right balance of realism  one of the best examples of this can be  found in the fight between captain  america and iron man in 2016's captain  america civil war this scene works so  well for several reasons for starters it  uses incredible choreography and camera  work to show us all of the action at  once as well as the limitations of the  characters in the space where they're  fighting it doesn't really rely on shaky  cam because it would be unnecessary in a  scene such as this instead we get  somewhat long takes that allow us to  feel some of the weight behind the blows  being landed and it makes us care about  the outcome of the fight because we're  concerned about the characters on both  sides we feel sorry for tony and  empathize with his desire for revenge  but we also understand steve's need to  save his friend bucky's life most of all  though this scene finds the perfect  balance of realism it's not completely  one-sided and tony doesn't just  immediately win with his super-powered  armor we see a fierce battle between  equals where their lives are all truly  on the line and yes it has a satisfying  ending because the underdog comes out on  top but it leaves us wanting further  resolution because these characters who  have been friends for years are now  separating themselves after such an  intense fight while there are tons of  examples of great superhero fight scenes  there are probably more not so great  ones out there for these fights to  really land with the audience creators  should look to iron man and caps fight  as a case study on how to use effective  cinematography get their audience  invested and find the right level of  realism now i'm sure there are more  aspects of superhero fights that can  make them great so leave your thoughts  in the comment section below also check  out the video on your screen for some  different content don't forget to like  and subscribe and thanks for watching  rocket riley

